As a Devsecops Everyone needs to know the 
Relationship and workflow between all these tools:

1. Shell Scripting
Role: Foundation for automation.
Use: Write scripts to automate repetitive tasks (starting services, cleaning logs, provisioning servers).
Relation: Acts as the glue between tools — you can trigger Ansible playbooks, Terraform commands, or Docker builds via scripts.

2. Ansible
• Role: Configuration management & automation.
• Use: Install packages, configure servers, manage users.
• Relation: Often triggered by shell scripts or CI/CD pipelines. Works well with Terraform (infra provisioning first, then Ansible config)

3. Terraform
• Role: Infrastructure as Code (IaC).
• Use: Provision cloud resources (AWS EC2, VPCs, Kubernetes clusters).
• Relation: Terraform sets up infra → Ansible configures it → Docker/Kubernetes deploy apps on it.

4. Docker
• Role: Containerization.
• Use: Package apps with dependencies into portable containers.
• Relation: Runs on infra provisioned by Terraform, configured by Ansible. Containers are later orchestrated by Kubernetes.

5. Kubernetes
• Role: Container orchestration.
• Use: Manage scaling, networking, and deployment of Docker containers.
• Relation: Terraform can provision the cluster, Ansible can configure nodes, Jenkins CI/CD can deploy apps into Kubernetes.

6. Jenkins
• Role: CI/CD automation.
• Use: Build, test, and deploy pipelines.
• Relation: Jenkins pipelines can:
• Run Terraform to provision infra
• Trigger Ansible for configuration
• Build Docker images
• Deploy to Kubernetes

🔄 Putting It All Together (Workflow Example)
1. Shell Script kicks off the pipeline.
2. Jenkins runs the CI/CD pipeline.
3. Terraform provisions cloud infra (servers, clusters).
4. Ansible configures servers (install Docker, set up networking).
5. Docker builds and packages the app.
6. Kubernetes deploys and manages containers.
7. Jenkins monitors and redeploys on changes

📊 Visual Flow (Simplified)

